  "obligation": "G110 -- per-kernel grid, TG, occupancy and register pressure",
  "schema": "hawking.nos.nongemv_occupancy_census.v1",
  "what_max_total_threads_measures": "Metal caps a threadgroup at 1024 threads. A pipeline reporting less is REGISTER-LIMITED, and the shortfall is the register pressure this acceptance calls load-bearing.",
  "verdict": {
    "no_kernel_is_register_limited": "All 20 kernels report maxTotalThreadsPerThreadgroup = 1024, the Metal maximum. NOT ONE is register-limited, and that includes the r8k8 and r16k4 tilings specifically.",
    "a_standing_ledger_claim_is_REFUTED": "This obligation's own evidence states: 'Register pressure is already known to be load-bearing -- the R x K tiling spilled at R=8 -- so this is not optional.' Measured directly, r8k1, r8k8, r16k1 and r16k4 all report 1024. THE R=8 AND R=16 REGRESSIONS ARE NOT REGISTER SPILLS.",
    "what_actually_limits_them": "Grid width, not threadgroup width. R rows share one threadgroup, so a tiling of R divides the threadgroup COUNT by R. On k_proj's 1024 rows: R=1 gives 512 threadgroups and fills all 60 cores; R=16 gives 32 threadgroups and leaves 28 cores idle. That is exactly the shape G109 measured -- small organs getting worse as R rises -- and it is an occupancy-across-cores problem rather than a registers-per-thread one.",
    "the_owed_structural_data": "Every non-GEMV kernel dispatches at 6% to 25% of the threads its own pipeline would allow: 64 or 256 against a permitted 1024. The headroom is 4x to 16x and nothing in the hardware is preventing it -- the dispatch geometry is a choice, and on this evidence a conservative one.",
    "what_this_does_NOT_say": "maxTotalThreadsPerThreadgroup is a compile-time pipeline property. It proves no kernel is REGISTER-limited; it does not prove that raising TG would be faster, because occupancy is not throughput -- G094 measured a 5.8x traffic swing producing zero rank correlation with time, and G111 measured argmax transferring 0 of a 25.9x isolated win. This census RANKS candidates for G111 to retile; it does not predict their end-to-end effect."
  },
